# Guardrails

These apply to every change, in both backend and frontend.

## Git and review

- Work on a feature branch. Never commit directly to `main`.
- Never push, force push, rewrite history, or merge without explicit approval from the developer.
- One logical change per commit. Show the diff summary before committing.
- Do not mark work done until the verification commands in the relevant skill pass, or the failure is reported.

## Secrets and data

- Never read, print, or edit `.env`, API keys, or tokens. Configuration examples go in `.env.example` with empty values.
- Never hardcode credentials, connection strings with passwords, or real tenant data.
- Sample leases are synthetic. Do not add real lease documents or personal data to the repository.
- Do not log PDF text, extracted field values, or API keys.

## Trust model (do not weaken)

- Raw provider output never writes to the `leases` table; only values with `evidence_found` do.
- Human and policy approval both go through `apply_approval`. Do not add a second approval path.
- Policy and agent failures fail closed to manual review or a block.
- Feature flags only tighten from defaults to property level.
- Fixture selection uses content hash or `sample_key`, never filename.
- Every query, RAG search, and MCP tool call is scoped to the organization.
- The LLM is used only for extraction. It must not decide approval, policy, or export.

## Scope control

- Change only what the task requires. Do not reformat unrelated files or upgrade dependencies unless asked.
- Do not edit existing Alembic migrations; add a new one.
- Do not delete or weaken tests to make a change pass. If a test is wrong, explain why before changing it.
- Changes to approval, policy, flags, evidence verification, or organization scoping need a test in the same commit.
- Keep `EXTRACTION_PROVIDER=fixture` as the default so tests and CI run without network access or secrets.

## Dependencies

- Ask before adding a dependency. Prefer the standard library or an existing package.
- Note the license of any new dependency. Copyleft licenses such as AGPL need explicit approval.
